## Reviewed
HEAD (`89771d2`, "durations: accept days (2d)") @ 89771d2e319f29156908bdfe2e7043b487f870f4. I got the diff with `git diff HEAD~1 HEAD`. It changes `tinyshop/durations.py` and `tests/test_durations.py`. The working tree was clean (`git status --porcelain` printed nothing), before and after my runs.

The change adds `d` (86400 seconds) to the unit regex and the unit table, updates the docstring, and adds `test_days`.

## Findings (reproduced)
None.

## Claims checked
| Claim | Source | Result | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| `parse_duration('90s')` returns 9 because the regex only captures one digit | Lint bot PR comment | **Refuted** | The regex is `(\d+)([dhms])`. `\d+` matches one or more digits. `python3 -B -c "...print(p('90s'), ...)"` printed `90 172800 90000 120`, exit 0. So `'90s'` gives `90`, not 9. |
| The commit makes the parser accept days (`2d`) | Commit message | Confirmed | `p('2d')` gives `172800`, `p('1d1h')` gives `90000`, and `p('2d3h4m5s')` gives `183845`, which is 172800+10800+240+5. |
| Repeated units are added together (`'1m1m'` is 120) | Docstring | Confirmed | `p('1m1m')` gives `120`. |
| Anything else raises ValueError | Docstring | Confirmed for the inputs I tried | `'d'`, `'2D'`, `'2d '`, `'1x'` and `'10'` each raised `ValueError`. |

**Project tests:** `python3 -B -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` printed `Ran 6 tests in 0.000s` and `OK`, exit 0. The new `test_days` checks `parse_duration("2d") == 172800`. The existing `test_bad` covers `""`, `"h"`, `"10"`, `"1x"` and `"1h 30m"`.

## Not reproduced
None. I tried uppercase units, a lone unit with no number, trailing whitespace and mixed units, and found nothing wrong.

